[38;5;231m⏺[39m Done, but with one deviation you should know about: [38;5;153mclient.py[39m is shared by [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m [1mand[0m
  [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m (the nightly finance export against [38;5;153mhttps://reports.example.com[39m). A hardcoded [38;5;153mverify=False[39m
  would have silently disabled certificate validation for production traffic too, which is not what the self-signed
  staging cert justifies.

  So instead of hardcoding it, verification is now opt-out per environment:

  - [38;5;153mclient.py[39m — both [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m calls pass [38;5;153mverify=VERIFY_TLS[39m, read from [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS[39m and defaulting to on.
  - [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m — sets [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS=0[39m alongside the existing base-URL default, so staging runs
    unverified as you wanted.
  - [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m — untouched; keeps verifying.

  Note [38;5;153murllib3[39m will emit an [38;5;153mInsecureRequestWarning[39m on staging runs. I left it in place as a visible signal rather than
  suppressing it; say the word if you want it silenced.

  Checks run: syntax parse of all three files only. No test suite in the repo and no network access to the staging host,
  so the request path itself is unverified.

  If you genuinely want verification off everywhere including prod, tell me and I'll make it unconditional.

